WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.104
History and navigation artifacts are often the first browser evidence people think about.

2
00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:17.044
They can include URLs, page titles, visit times, visit counts, typed URL indicators, redirects, referrers, tab restoration data, and session restore artifacts.

3
00:00:17.044 --> 00:00:23.465
These records can help reconstruct web activity, but each field needs careful interpretation.

4
00:00:23.465 --> 00:00:30.838
A history entry may indicate that a browser recorded a visit to a URL at a time, but the path to that entry may vary.

5
00:00:30.838 --> 00:00:43.284
The user may have typed the address, clicked a link, followed a search result, opened a bookmark, restored a session, triggered a redirect, loaded a frame, or received a synchronized tab.

6
00:00:43.284 --> 00:00:50.339
Visit count can suggest repeated access, but it does not always map cleanly to meaningful user attention.

7
00:00:50.339 --> 00:00:58.980
Typed URLs can be useful because they may indicate deliberate entry into the address bar, but even typed indicators need caution.

8
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:07.701
Autocomplete, pasted text, browser behavior, restored state, or data imported from another profile may affect interpretation.

9
00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:19.988
Referrers and redirects can help show navigation path, but they may also reflect advertising networks, identity providers, single sign-on flows, or embedded application behavior.

10
00:01:19.988 --> 00:01:26.727
Tab restoration and session artifacts can show what was open or recoverable after a browser restart.

11
00:01:26.727 --> 00:01:31.324
That can be valuable in incident response or user activity analysis.

12
00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:38.697
But an open tab is not the same as active reading, and a restored session may preserve state from a previous time.

13
00:01:38.697 --> 00:01:47.893
Investigators should avoid saying a user intentionally viewed content unless supporting evidence justifies that level of confidence.

14
00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:52.729
History analysis becomes stronger when correlated with other records.

15
00:01:52.729 --> 00:02:06.681
File downloads, endpoint logs, DNS records, proxy logs, web application logs, email timestamps, and user activity can all help distinguish manual navigation from automatic loading or background activity.

16
00:02:06.681 --> 00:02:12.151
The browser history is a starting point for a timeline, not the timeline by itself.
